      "text": "To solve the system of linear equations, we can use the substitution or elimination method. I'll use the elimination method in this case.\n\nCombine the two equations by adding them together:\n\n(3x - 5y = -11) + (7x +2y = -12), multiplying both sides by 11 and 6 respectively:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5y +      7x + 2y = -11 + 6\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n10x - 3y = -5\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for x in terms of y:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-5 + 3y}{10}\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute this expression for x back into one of the original equations (let's use the first one):\n\n\\[\n3(\\frac{-5 + 3y}{10}) - 5y = -11\n\\]\n\nExpand and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-15 + 9y}{10} - 5y = -11\n\\]\n\nMultiply through by 10 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n-15 + 9y - 50y = -110\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n-41y = -95\n\\]\n\nDivide by -41:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{95}{41}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of y, substitute it back into our expression for x:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-5 + 3(\\frac{95}{41})}{10}\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\frac{-175 + 285}{41}}{10}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\frac{110}{41}}{10}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{110}{410}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{11}{41}\n\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is (x,y) = (11/41, 95/41). To express this as an ordered pair:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{11}{41}, \\frac{95}{41}\\right)}\n\\]",
